    "explanation": "Q1 and Q2 differ because Q1 uses an INNER JOIN with a subquery that filters table2 rows where ALL c6 values for each c3 group fall within Jan-Mar 2019 (MIN(c6) >= '2019-01-01' AND MAX(c6) <= '2019-03-31'), while Q2 uses a RIGHT JOIN that includes table2 rows with c6 in that range, then excludes c3 values that have ANY c6 outside that range. The key difference is Q1 requires ALL c6 values for a c3 to be within the range, while Q2 excludes c3 values that have ANY c6 outside the range, but includes c3 values that have some rows within the range and some rows outside it (which get filtered out by the WHERE clause).",
    "counterexample_dsp": "The difference occurs when a c3 value in table2 has at least one row with c6 within Jan-Mar 2019 and at least one row with c6 outside that range. Q1 excludes such c3 values because not ALL c6 values are within the range (fails the HAVING condition on MIN/MAX). Q2 initially includes such c3 values via the RIGHT JOIN (for rows with c6 in range), but then excludes them via the NOT IN subquery (which catches c3 values with ANY c6 outside the range). However, the WHERE clause in Q2 filters to only rows with c6 in range, so the RIGHT JOIN produces rows for such c3 values, but the NOT IN subquery removes them entirely, resulting in no output for that c3. Thus, both queries output nothing for such c3 values, making them equivalent in this case. The actual divergence requires a scenario where Q2 outputs rows for a c3 that Q1 excludes. This happens when a c3 has NO rows in table2 with c6 in the range (so Q1 excludes it), but the RIGHT JOIN in Q2 might still include it from table1 if there's a match, and the NOT IN subquery doesn't exclude it (if it has no rows outside the range either). Specifically: insert a c3 into table1 that has no matching rows in table2 at all. Q1 excludes it (no match in subquery). Q2: RIGHT JOIN includes it (from table1), WHERE c6 condition fails for all rows (since no rows), but RIGHT JOIN keeps table1 rows with NULLs from table2; the NOT IN subquery doesn't exclude it (no rows in table2), so it outputs the row with NULL c6? Wait, Q2 has WHERE c6 >= '2019-01-01' AND c6 < '2019-04-01', which will filter out rows with NULL c6. So Q2 also excludes it. Thus, to get a difference, we need a c3 that has rows in table2, but NONE with c6 in the range, and ALL rows with c6 outside the range. Q1 excludes it (no rows in subquery). Q2: RIGHT JOIN includes rows from table2 with c6 outside range, but WHERE filters them out (c6 not in range), leaving no matches; the NOT IN subquery excludes it (since it has rows with c6 outside range), so Q2 also excludes it. Hmm. Let's re-examine: Q2's NOT IN subquery is: NOT c3 IN (SELECT c3 FROM table2 WHERE c6 < '2019-01-01' OR c6 >= '2019-04-01'). This excludes c3 values that have ANY row with c6 outside the range. If a c3 has ALL rows outside the range, it will be excluded by NOT IN. So both queries exclude it. The divergence requires a c3 that Q1 excludes but Q2 includes. That means: for Q1, the c3 must not satisfy the HAVING condition (i.e., not all c6 in range). For Q2, it must satisfy the WHERE and NOT IN conditions. WHERE requires c6 in range for the joined row. NOT IN requires no rows with c6 outside range. So if a c3 has some rows with c6 in range and some outside, Q1 excludes, Q2 excludes (by NOT IN). If a c3 has all rows in range, both include. If a c3 has no rows in range, both exclude. Wait, but Q2 uses RIGHT JOIN, so if a c3 in table1 has no matching rows in table2, it will be included with NULLs, but WHERE filters out NULL c6, so excluded. So no difference. However, consider NULL handling: if c6 is NULL, it fails both WHERE conditions (c6 >= '2019-01-01' is false for NULL). So NULL c6 rows are filtered out. But the NOT IN subquery: WHERE c6 < '2019-01-01' OR c6 >= '2019-04-01' – NULL fails both, so NULL rows are not included in the subquery. Thus, if a c3 has only NULL c6 rows, it won't be excluded by NOT IN. So: insert a c3 into table1 and table2 with rows where c6 is NULL. Q1: subquery groups by c3, MIN(c6) and MAX(c6) are NULL for the group, so MIN(c6) >= '2019-01-01' is NULL (false in HAVING), and MAX(c6) <= '2019-03-31' is NULL (false), so the group is excluded. Q2: RIGHT JOIN includes rows with NULL c6, WHERE filters them out (c6 not in range), so no rows from table2 match; but RIGHT JOIN keeps table1 rows with NULLs? Actually, RIGHT JOIN table2 AS S USING (c3) – this keeps all rows from table2, and matching rows from table1. For rows where table2.c6 is NULL, WHERE filters them out. For c3 values that have only NULL c6 rows in table2, no rows