n fails (UNKNOWN treated as FALSE). So Q1 excludes. Q2 also excludes (not in CTE1). So still equivalent. Wait, maybe the difference is when table2 has rows for a c3, but all have c6 NULL. Then Q1 excludes (as above). Q2: CTE1 has no such c3 (NULL not between dates). CTE2 has no such c3 (NULL not < or > dates). So not in result. Equivalent. Let's reconsider the original thought: The key is that Q1's HAVING requires MIN(c6) >= '2019-01-01' AND MAX(c6) <= '2019-03-31'. This means ALL c6 values for that c3 must be within the range (since min and max are within). Q2 requires: there exists at least one c6 within range (CTE1) AND no c6 outside range (CTE2 IS NULL). This is logically equivalent to: all c6 values are within range? Not exactly: If there are no rows for a c3, both conditions fail. If there are rows, Q2 requires at least one within and none outside. This is equivalent to: all rows are within range AND there is at least one row. Q1 requires all rows are within range (and there is at least one row, since grouping on c3 produces a group only if there is at least one row). So they seem equivalent. But wait, what about NULL dates? If a c3 has rows with c6 within range and also rows with c6 NULL. Q1: MIN and MAX ignore NULLs, so MIN and MAX are within range (since non-NULL rows are within). So HAVING condition passes, includes c3. Q2: CTE1 includes c3 (due to within-range row). CTE2: c6 NULL does not satisfy c6 < '2019-01-01' OR c6 > '2019-03-31' (NULL yields UNKNOWN), so not in CTE2. So LEFT JOIN doesn't match, WHERE CTE2.c3 IS NULL keeps it. So both include. Still equivalent. Hmm, maybe the difference is in handling of duplicates? DISTINCT in CTE1 and CTE2 removes duplicates, but that shouldn't affect logic since we care about existence. Let's construct a case: c3=1 has two rows in table2: one with c6='2019-02-01' (within), another with c6='2019-02-01' (same date, duplicate). Q1: GROUP BY c3 gives one group, MIN=MAX='2019-02-01', within range, includes. Q2: CTE1 ge