des. No difference. Wait, the issue is that Q2's SUB filters RNK IN (1,2), so it only considers the first two rows per group by DENSE_RANK. If the consecutive pair is at ranks 2 and 3, Q2 misses it. But Q1 only considers the earliest date, so it also misses if earliest date not part of consecutive pair. So both miss in that case. To trigger difference, we need Q1 to count a group that Q2 does not, or vice versa. Let's design: Group where earliest date has a consecutive date, so Q1 counts it. But the first two ranked dates are not consecutive, so Q2 does not count it. For example, dates: 2023-01-01 (earliest, rank1), 2023-01-02 (rank2), 2023-01-03 (rank3). Here, earliest date 2023-01-01 has consecutive date 2023-01-02, so Q1 counts. Ranks 1 and 2 are 2023-01-01 and 2023-01-02, which are consecutive, so Q2 also counts. No difference. To make ranks 1 and 2 not consecutive, but earliest date still has consecutive date, we need the consecutive date to the earliest date to not be among the first two ranks. But DENSE_RANK orders by c2, so the earliest date is rank1, and the next date in order is rank2. If earliest date has a consecutive date, that date must be rank2 (since dates are ordered). So ranks 1 and 2 will always be consecutive if earliest date has a consecutive date. Therefore, Q1 and Q2 will always agree on groups where earliest date has a consecutive date. Difference can only occur when earliest date does not have a consecutive date. In that case, Q1 excludes the group. Q2 may include it if ranks 1 and 2 are consecutive (even though earliest date does not have consecutive date? But earliest date is rank1, so if ranks 1 and 2 are consecutive, then earliest date has consecutive date. Contradiction. So if earliest date has no consecutive date, then rank1 and rank2 cannot be consecutive. Thus, Q2 will also exclude. So they are equivalent? Let's test with data: c4=1, dates: 2023-01-01, 2023-01-03, 2023-01-04. Q1: earliest 2023-01-01, no date 2023-01-02, so exclude. Q